**Convention 11.2 (time, stopping and complete output).** The required
failure measure is an operationally timed output, not just the law of a
number recorded at terminal time. Execution must cause the actual first
failure at elapsed time $t$, with absolute clock $6+t$, make that branch
absorbing, and perform no later positive-carrier action on it. A live
branch must deliver its prescribed live output at elapsed time one.
A sampled timestamp or the scalar CP measure in a mathematical decoder
does not by itself implement this timing service. No new acquisition
interval is added.

The two nonzero side failure labels remain distinct; mode $0$ and mode
$13$ remain named zero sectors. Every old stopped archive stays in its
own branch. The source identity and epoch, calibrations, permissions,
controller updates and clock alignment keep their original contracts.
The complete live, failure and zero-sector reference identities are
required together, including complement and cross-support actions.
There is no conditioning on survival or acceptance and no rescaling by
old reach probabilities.

